id: decisions/040-one-naming-rule-with-a-ledger
title: "The naming invariant covers files, ids and case"
kind: decision-frame
record_shape: header
status: draft
authored_by: agent-on-behalf
authored_via: "Claude Code session on Dave's behalf, 2026-09-12; drafted from the JUDGMENTS.md reversal axis 'naming-convention-uniformity'; recommendation is an attributed inference and ratifies nothing"
version: "1.0.0"
updated: 2026-09-12
template_ref: templates/decision.yawn
principal_ref: principal:dave

axis: naming-convention-uniformity
axis_gloss: "whether every path, key and file follows one casing and naming rule, or files may depart from it"
poles:
  a: uniform-naming-convention
  b: per-file-naming-freedom
split: leaning
draft_status: full
leverage: 6.7
leverage_formula: "judgments_resolved(8) x split_weight(0.7) x authored_conflict_bonus(1.2)"

question: >
  Does one naming rule cover everything — file names, record ids, dated names,
  extension case — or only the extension and product tokens it already proves?

why_it_matters: >
  The authored invariant (.yawn lowercase, YAWN uppercase, no .ion/.yon aliases)
  has a validator and tests, but it does not check case, so the uppercase
  MASTER-CONTROL.YAWN root that four of five scheduled bots run against passes;
  meanwhile two constitutional files scream, three id shapes coexist in
  examples/, and the file that argues paths carry no identity is the only one
  with a URN id. Once answered, a bot creating a file knows one rule for its
  name, its id and its case, and a child Yawn knows whether uppercase means
  anything.

state_snapshot:
  description: >
    5 judgments sit at uniform-naming-convention (3 authored) and 3 at
    per-file-naming-freedom (0 authored). The repository asserts both directions
    on this axis.
  attributed_to: JUDGMENTS.md
  epistemic_status: observed
  source_refs:
    - JUDGMENTS.md
    - TAXONOMY.md

lacuna:
  kind: unselected_choice
  description: >
    Whether uppercase filenames were meant to mark constitutional documents, and
    whether MASTER-CONTROL.YAWN is a real directory on Dave's machine that cannot
    be renamed.

options:
  - name: A
    description: >
      rule: One naming rule everywhere: kebab-case filenames, unquoted path-shaped
      ids equal to the file's path without extension, subject-first names with the
      date as a suffix, snake_case keys, lowercase extension in every tracked path
      and every workspace or source path named in a record. The three uppercase
      files are renamed, the URN and singular ids normalised, the one date-first
      record renamed.
    cost: "Renames core/RELATIONSHIP_FIRST_AGENT_ARENA.yawn and references/RELATIONSHIP_FIRST_RESEARCH_BRAID.yawn and every link to them; changes ids in examples/ and core/canonical-extension.yawn; either lowercases the MASTER-CONTROL.YAWN root on Dave's machine or breaks four automations' workspace paths."
    risk: "Renaming the constitutional files erases a signal (uppercase = foundational) that nobody wrote down but may be intended; the workspace root may not be renameable."
    reversibility: true
    proof_needed: "Extend scripts/validate-canonical-extension.mjs: fail on any .YAWN/.Yawn in tracked paths or content, any non-kebab .yawn filename, any id: not equal to its path; today it fails on at least eleven files."
    evidence_for:
      - "J0214"
      - "J0326"
      - "J0657"
      - "J0892"
      - "J0927"
  - name: B
    description: >
      rule: Only the extension token and product name are governed. Filename case,
      id shape, key case and dated-name order are per-author choices and are not
      checked.
    cost: "Softens canonical.case in core/canonical-extension.yawn to advisory and drops the 'source path' clause from its invariant."
    risk: "A fourth id shape and a third case convention arrive with the next authoring batch, and a bot that indexes by id or path has to guess."
    reversibility: true
    proof_needed: "None can enforce freedom; the record would be moving canonical.case out of the invariant list."
    evidence_for:
      - "J0272"
      - "J0387"
      - "J0916"
  - name: C
    description: >
      rule: The naming invariant extends to filename case, id shape (id equals path
      without extension) and dated-name order (subject first, date suffix) for every
      new file, and the validator checks extension case in tracked paths and in
      workspace or source paths named by records. Existing outliers are
      grandfathered by name in the validator with a ledger that may only shrink: the
      two SCREAMING constitutional files (or, if Dave says uppercase marks
      constitutional status, that is written into core/what-is-a-yawn.yawn
      canonical_naming as the one permitted exception), .github/ISSUE_TEMPLATE which
      is GitHub's own convention, the yawn:core:canonical-extension URN id, the four
      singular and URN example ids, the one date-first record, and the
      MASTER-CONTROL.YAWN external root marked reachability: operator-only.
    cost: "One ledger in scripts/validate-canonical-extension.mjs, one paragraph in core/what-is-a-yawn.yawn canonical_naming, no renames."
    risk: "The ledger is the rule's escape hatch; if it never shrinks, C is B with paperwork."
    reversibility: true
    proof_needed: "Extend scripts/validate-canonical-extension.mjs with three checks (extension case including drive-letter paths, kebab-case filenames, id equals path) each with a frozen grandfather set and a ceiling, following tests/status-vocabulary.test.mjs; move canonical.case from not_covered to covers in core/canonical-extension.yawn proof."
    evidence_for:
      - "J0214"
      - "J0892"
      - "J0927"
      - "J0326"
      - "J0657"

recommendation:
  option_ref: C
  confidence: 0.75
  epistemic_status: inferred
  authority: none
  reasoning: >
    The uniform side holds all three authored judgments and two proof-attached
    ones; every freedom-side judgment is revealed and unremarked. The stated
    invariant already includes case and source paths (GROUND_TRUTH FACT 20) and
    the in-flight proof block admits the validator does not check them, so the
    honest move is to make the validator cover what the record already claims.
    Renaming the constitutional files (A) is the only expensive part and the only
    place a meaning might be lost, which is why the exception is grandfathered
    rather than removed; the shrinking-ledger pattern is the repo's own
    established mechanism three times over.

target_condition:
  mode: decide
  proposed_by: agent-on-behalf
  affected_principals:
    - principal:dave
  ratification_status: proposed
  sufficient_when:
    - "canonical.case moves from not_covered to covers in core/canonical-extension.yawn proof and scripts/validate-canonical-extension.mjs rejects an uppercase extension in a tracked path or a record-named workspace path."
    - "core/what-is-a-yawn.yawn canonical_naming states the id and filename rule, and whether uppercase filenames mean anything."
    - "Each outlier is renamed or grandfathered by name in a ledger with a ceiling, and the ledger has not grown at the next inventory."
    - "npm run validate passes with the new checks."

choice:
  selected_by: ""
  option_ref: ""
  authority_ref: ""

proof:
  covers:
    - target_condition
    - options
  falsifier: >
    A new file lands with an uppercase name, a URN or singular id, or a date-first
    name, and validate passes without a ledger change.
  status: proposed

resolves:
  - "J0214"
  - "J0326"
  - "J0657"
  - "J0892"
  - "J0927"
  - "J0272"
  - "J0387"
  - "J0916"

boundary:
  - "confidence cannot ratify an objective; this record proposes and Dave decides"
  - "the REVEALED judgments cited here describe what artifacts do, not what Dave chose"

source:
  template: templates/decision.yawn
  axis: TAXONOMY.md
  inventory: JUDGMENTS.md
